#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdlib>

// Compute the maximum contiguous sum over compressed alternating-sign segments.
// Each segment groups consecutive numbers with the same sign, and its value is
// the sum of the absolute values of those numbers.
long long maxAlternatingSegmentSum(const std::vector<int>& v) {
    if (v.empty()) return 0;
    
    // Build compressed segment sums.
    std::vector<long long> segments;
    long long current = 0;
    bool current_positive = (v[0] > 0);
    for (int val : v) {
        bool is_positive = (val > 0);
        if (is_positive != current_positive) {
            segments.push_back(current);
            current = 0;
            current_positive = is_positive;
        }
        current += std::llabs(static_cast<long long>(val));
    }
    segments.push_back(current); // push last segment
    
    // Apply Kadane's algorithm on the segment values.
    long long best = segments[0];
    long long current_sum = segments[0];
    for (size_t i = 1; i < segments.size(); ++i) {
        current_sum = std::max(segments[i], current_sum + segments[i]);
        best = std::max(best, current_sum);
    }
    return best;
}
// The core idea is to compress the array into a list of segment sums where each segment groups consecutive numbers of the same sign. Each segment’s value is the sum of the absolute values of its elements in that run. After compression, we have a sequence of alternating positive and negative segment values. The task reduces to finding the maximum subarray sum over this compressed sequence, but with the twist that we can choose any contiguous block, regardless of starting sign. This is exactly the classic maximum subarray sum (Kadane’s algorithm) applied to the compressed values. However, we must be careful: the original problem’s intended solution in the snippet uses a sliding window approach that starts from either a positive or negative segment, but it effectively computes the maximum subarray sum on the compressed list. To be safe and correct, we can just apply Kadane’s algorithm directly on the segment values. Edge cases: if the entire array has one sign, the compressed list has one element, and the answer is that element’s absolute sum. If the array alternates signs every element, each segment is just the absolute value of a single number. Time complexity: O(n) for compression and O(k) for Kadane where k is number of segments (k ≤ n). Space O(k) for storing the segment values (or we could do it in one pass, but for clarity we store). Overall O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space in worst case.
